Why: mainline read-modify-writes DMAC_CFG - the single controller-wide register
holding DMAC_EN (bit 0) and INT_EN (bit 1) - from two contexts with nothing
serializing them. dw_axi_dma_interrupt() clears INT_EN on entry and restores it
on exit; axi_chan_block_xfer_start() sets DMAC_EN on every channel start. The
per-channel chan->vc.lock does not cover the handler's writes, so on SMP the two
interleave and one update is lost. The handler's completion callback wakes the
submitting thread on the other CPU while INT_EN is still 0, which is the exact
window that makes the bad interleave likely rather than theoretical: that thread
reads DMAC_CFG with INT_EN clear, and its write-back of DMAC_EN lands after the
handler has restored INT_EN, clearing it again.

Nothing sets INT_EN except the handler's own exit path, so the loss is
self-latching. The controller stays enabled and keeps completing transfers, and
per-channel INTSTATUS keeps being set, but no interrupt ever reaches the GIC
again: every dmaengine client blocks until its timeout, forever, controller-wide,
with no driver message of any kind.

Seen twice on the Proxima-9311 goggle after ~200k interrupts of a 60 fps compose
blit. Live, in the failed state: DMAC_CFG 0x00000001 (DMAC_EN set, INT_EN clear),
DMAC_INTSTATUS 0x00000003 (channels 0 and 1 pending), dw_axi_dmac IRQ counts
frozen, dmesg clean. Writing INT_EN back by hand restored the engine on the spot
- the compositor went from 4091.6 ms per frame (four 1 s dmaengine timeouts) back
to 16.6 ms with no restart.

Fix is a leaf spinlock (chip->cfg_lock, added by 0500) around each of the four
DMAC_CFG helpers. None of them calls another, so there is nothing to nest.

--- a/drivers/dma/dw-axi-dmac/dw-axi-dmac-platform.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/dw-axi-dmac/dw-axi-dmac-platform.c
@@ -122,40 +122,75 @@
 	axi_chan_iowrite32(chan, CH_CFG_H, cfg_hi);
 }
 
+/*
+ * ML (Artosyn): DMAC_CFG read-modify-write, serialized against every other one.
+ *
+ * DMAC_EN and INT_EN share this single controller-wide register, and the four
+ * helpers below are reached from both process context (a channel start, via
+ * axi_dma_enable) and hard IRQ context (dw_axi_dma_interrupt, which clears
+ * INT_EN on entry and restores it on exit). chan->vc.lock does not serialize
+ * them: it is per channel and the handler does not take it around those writes.
+ *
+ * On SMP the two therefore interleave and lose an update. The handler's
+ * completion callback wakes the submitting thread on the other CPU while INT_EN
+ * is still 0, so that thread reads DMAC_CFG with INT_EN clear, and its write
+ * back of DMAC_EN lands after the handler has restored INT_EN, clearing it
+ * again. Nothing sets INT_EN except the handler's own exit path, so once it is
+ * lost the controller never raises another interrupt: transfers still run and
+ * still set per-channel INTSTATUS, but no IRQ reaches the GIC, every
+ * dma_async_issue_pending waiter times out, and the failure is permanent,
+ * silent and controller-wide. Observed on a Proxima-9311 after ~200k
+ * interrupts: DMAC_CFG read 0x1 (DMAC_EN set, INT_EN clear) with
+ * DMAC_INTSTATUS 0x3 pending; writing INT_EN back by hand resumed the engine.
+ *
+ * A single leaf lock is enough: none of the four calls another.
+ */
 static inline void axi_dma_disable(struct axi_dma_chip *chip)
 {
+	unsigned long flags;
 	u32 val;
 
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 	val = axi_dma_ioread32(chip, DMAC_CFG);
 	val &= ~DMAC_EN_MASK;
 	axi_dma_iowrite32(chip, DMAC_CFG, val);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 }
 
 static inline void axi_dma_enable(struct axi_dma_chip *chip)
 {
+	unsigned long flags;
 	u32 val;
 
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 	val = axi_dma_ioread32(chip, DMAC_CFG);
 	val |= DMAC_EN_MASK;
 	axi_dma_iowrite32(chip, DMAC_CFG, val);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 }
 
 static inline void axi_dma_irq_disable(struct axi_dma_chip *chip)
 {
+	unsigned long flags;
 	u32 val;
 
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 	val = axi_dma_ioread32(chip, DMAC_CFG);
 	val &= ~INT_EN_MASK;
 	axi_dma_iowrite32(chip, DMAC_CFG, val);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 }
 
 static inline void axi_dma_irq_enable(struct axi_dma_chip *chip)
 {
+	unsigned long flags;
 	u32 val;
 
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 	val = axi_dma_ioread32(chip, DMAC_CFG);
 	val |= INT_EN_MASK;
 	axi_dma_iowrite32(chip, DMAC_CFG, val);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&chip->cfg_lock, flags);
 }
 
 static inline void axi_chan_irq_disable(struct axi_dma_chan *chan, u32 irq_mask)
@@ -1484,6 +1519,8 @@
 	chip->dw = dw;
 	chip->dev = &pdev->dev;
 	chip->dw->hdata = hdata;
+	/* ML (Artosyn): guards the DMAC_CFG read-modify-write, see above. */
+	spin_lock_init(&chip->cfg_lock);
 
 	chip->regs = devm_platform_ioremap_resource(pdev, 0);
 	if (IS_ERR(chip->regs))
